course: dictionary
lesson: 3158
title: Emigrate: leaving your country to live elsewhere
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: To emigrate means to leave your own country and go to live permanently in another country. It is a verb, so it can change: emigrate, emigrates, emigrated, emigrating. You emigrate from the country you leave and to the country where you settle.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. In «many to America» the words around emigrated tell you that people left their homes and went to live in a new place. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence: «many people left their own country to live in America» still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Do not mix up emigrate and immigrate. People who emigrate move away from a country, and people who immigrate move into a new one. The same journey is described from two sides: the family emigrates from Ireland and immigrates into America.
fact: Emigrate is a verb: emigrate, emigrates, emigrated, emigrating.
fact: «Emigrate from» names the place you leave; «emigrate to» names the place you go.
fact: People emigrate for jobs, for study, to be near family, or to escape danger.
fact: In history, many people emigrated from Ireland, Britain and Italy to the USA, Canada, Australia and New Zealand.
fact: During the Great Famine of 1845 to 1852, over a million people emigrated from Ireland, and about a million died.
exercise: What does emigrate mean? | To move house within the same town | To learn a new language at school | To visit another country for a short holiday | *To leave your own country to go and live permanently in another country
exercise: «Many people emigrated to America.» Which word tells you where they went? | emigrated | *America | people | many
exercise: What is an immigrant? | A person who grows potatoes | *A person who moves into a new country to live there | A person who leaves their country for a holiday | A type of official document
test: Which sentence uses emigrate correctly? | Her family emigrated to the park for a picnic. | Her family emigrated across the road to the shop. | *Her family emigrated from Italy to Canada to find work. | Her family emigrated the bus to school this morning.
test: What is a visa? | A plane ticket | A school exam | A type of potato | *Official permission to come and stay in a country
exam: Which meaning fits «During the Great Famine, over a million people emigrated»? | To cross a river by boat | To work in a bakery | To starve from hunger | *To leave your country to live permanently in another country
